(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("python-ops",{"python-ops/project-structure-packaging-and-virtual-environments":{"html":"\u003cp>A script that works only on your laptop is a liability. A small package with an isolated environment and pinned dependencies installs the same way everywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A src layout and pyproject.toml\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Virtual environments and why they matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning and locking dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Entry points that become commands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproducible installs in CI and containers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put code in \u003ccode>src/mytool/\u003c/code> with tests in \u003ccode>tests/\u003c/code> and metadata in \u003ccode>pyproject.toml\u003c/code>. The src layout stops tests from importing the working directory by accident, so they test what is installed. Keep a short README that says how to install and run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-environments\">Virtual environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A virtual environment gives each project its own interpreter and packages, so one tool upgrading a library does not break another. Create it with \u003ccode>python -m venv .venv\u003c/code>, activate it and install into it. Never install project dependencies into the system Python on a server.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pinning\">Pinning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A range such as \u003ccode>requests&gt;=2\u003c/code> lets a new release change behaviour overnight. Libraries declare ranges, applications lock exact versions with a tool such as pip-tools, uv or Poetry, and commit the lock file. Update on purpose, run the tests, and read the changelog.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"entry-points\">Entry points\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Declare \u003ccode>[project.scripts] mytool = &quot;mytool.cli:main&quot;\u003c/code> and the installer creates a \u003ccode>mytool\u003c/code> command. Operators then run a named command instead of \u003ccode>python path/to/script.py\u003c/code>, and it works from any directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reproducible-installs\">Reproducible installs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In CI and container builds install from the lock file with hashes where possible, use a slim base image, and copy the lock file before the source so the dependency layer is cached. Record the Python version and fail the build if it is different.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"src&gt;venv;venv&gt;lock;lock&gt;ci\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Project structure, packaging and virtual environments\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Source package\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"venv\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Virtual environment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lock\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lock file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CI and image\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Source package to Virtual environment; 2, Virtual environment to Lock file; 3, Lock file to CI and image.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Source package \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> src/mytool.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Imports from cwd.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"venv\">\u003cdt>Virtual environment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Isolated packages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> System Python.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lock\">\u003cdt>Lock file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exact versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpinned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>CI and image \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Installs from lock.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-tool-that-broke-after-a-library-release\">Incident: A tool that broke after a library release\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scheduled job failed because a dependency released a breaking version overnight.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pip list --format=freeze | grep -i requests\npython -c &quot;print('job failed: unexpected keyword argument')&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pip list --format=freeze | grep -i requests\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">requests==3.0.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(&#x27;job failed: unexpected keyword argument&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The requirements file used a loose range, so the next build picked up a new major release. The team locked exact versions, committed the lock file and used a scheduled update job that runs the tests before any bump is merged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Installing into the system Python\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loose version ranges in applications\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not committing the lock file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripts that depend on the current directory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No pinned Python version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"virtual-environments","text":"Virtual environments"},{"id":"pinning","text":"Pinning"},{"id":"entry-points","text":"Entry points"},{"id":"reproducible-installs","text":"Reproducible installs"},{"id":"incident-a-tool-that-broke-after-a-library-release","text":"Incident: A tool that broke after a library release"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/"},{"t":"venv","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html"},{"t":"argparse","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html"}],"text":"A script that works only on your laptop is a liability. A small package with an isolated environment and pinned dependencies installs the same way everywhere. What you will learn A src layout and pyproject.toml Virtual environments and why they matter Pinning and locking dependencies Entry points that become commands Reproducible installs in CI and containers Layout Put code in src/mytool/ with tests in tests/ and metadata in pyproject.toml . The src layout stops tests from importing the working directory by accident, so they test what is installed. Keep a short README that says how to install and run. Virtual environments A virtual environment gives each project its own interpreter and packages, so one tool upgrading a library does not break another. Create it with python -m venv .venv , activate it and install into it. Never install project dependencies into the system Python on a server. Pinning A range such as requests>=2 lets a new release change behaviour overnight. Libraries declare ranges, applications lock exact versions with a tool such as pip-tools, uv or Poetry, and commit the lock file. Update on purpose, run the tests, and read the changelog. Entry points Declare [project.scripts] mytool = \"mytool.cli:main\" and the installer creates a mytool command. Operators then run a named command instead of python path/to/script.py , and it works from any directory. Reproducible installs In CI and container builds install from the lock file with hashes where possible, use a slim base image, and copy the lock file before the source so the dependency layer is cached. Record the Python version and fail the build if it is different. Project structure, packaging and virtual environments Flow in order: 1, Source package to Virtual environment; 2, Virtual environment to Lock file; 3, Lock file to CI and image. Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Source package Step What it does: src/mytool. If it fails or is weak: Imports from cwd. Virtual environment Step What it does: Isolated packages. If it fails or is weak: System Python. Lock file Data store What it does: Exact versions. If it fails or is weak: Unpinned. CI and image Service What it does: Installs from lock. If it fails or is weak: Drift. Incident: A tool that broke after a library release Incident A scheduled job failed because a dependency released a breaking version overnight. $ pip list --format=freeze | grep -i requests requests==3.0.0 $ python -c \"print(&#x27;job failed: unexpected keyword argument&#x27;)\" (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The requirements file used a loose range, so the next build picked up a new major release. The team locked exact versions, committed the lock file and used a scheduled update job that runs the tests before any bump is merged. Common mistakes Installing into the system Python Loose version ranges in applications Not committing the lock file Scripts that depend on the current directory No pinned Python version"},"python-ops/cli-tools-with-argparse-click-and-typer":{"html":"\u003cp>Operators run your tool at bad moments. Clear arguments, safe defaults, a dry run and honest exit codes make it trustworthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Arguments, subcommands and help\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exit codes and error messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dry run and confirmation for dangerous actions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Machine readable output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing argparse, click or typer\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"arguments\">Arguments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good tool has subcommands such as \u003ccode>mytool backup run\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>mytool backup list\u003c/code>, options with defaults and help text for each. \u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code> in the standard library handles this without dependencies, which suits tools installed on servers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exit-codes\">Exit codes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Return 0 on success and a non zero code on failure so scripts and schedulers can react. Print errors to standard error, and make messages say what failed and what to do next. Do not print a stack trace for an expected problem such as a missing file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dry-run\">Dry run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Destructive tools need a \u003ccode>--dry-run\u003c/code> that prints what would change, and often a required \u003ccode>--yes\u003c/code> or a typed confirmation for production. Default to the safe option, and show the target environment in the output so mistakes are visible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"output\">Output\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Provide \u003ccode>--output json\u003c/code> for scripts alongside readable text for people. Write results to standard output and logs to standard error, so piping into other tools works. Keep field names stable, because other tools will depend on them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"libraries\">Libraries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>argparse\u003c/code> is enough for most tools. \u003ccode>click\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>typer\u003c/code> give less boilerplate, nicer help and testing helpers, at the cost of a dependency. Pick one per team and wrap your logic in plain functions so the CLI layer stays thin and easy to test.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"args&gt;plan;plan&gt;act;act&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">CLI tools with argparse, click and typer\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"args\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parse arguments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan and dry run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Apply\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exit code and output\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Parse arguments to Plan and dry run; 2, Plan and dry run to Apply; 3, Apply to Exit code and output.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"args\">\u003cdt>Parse arguments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validate input.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear help.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Plan and dry run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Show changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Apply \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Do the work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong target.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Exit code and output \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Report result.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Always zero.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-cleanup-tool-that-ran-against-production\">Incident: A cleanup tool that ran against production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer meant to test a cleanup in staging, but the default target was production.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mytool cleanup --older-than 30d\necho $?\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mytool cleanup --older-than 30d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">target: production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deleted 1,204 objects\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tool defaulted to production and had no dry run, so the first run was real. The team made the environment a required argument, added --dry-run as the default behaviour for destructive commands and printed the target in the first line of output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defaulting to production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Always returning exit code zero\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing logs and results on standard output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giant functions inside the CLI layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No help text\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"arguments","text":"Arguments"},{"id":"exit-codes","text":"Exit codes"},{"id":"dry-run","text":"Dry run"},{"id":"output","text":"Output"},{"id":"libraries","text":"Libraries"},{"id":"incident-a-cleanup-tool-that-ran-against-production","text":"Incident: A cleanup tool that ran against production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"argparse","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/argparse.html"},{"t":"click","u":"https://github.com/pallets/click"},{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"Operators run your tool at bad moments. Clear arguments, safe defaults, a dry run and honest exit codes make it trustworthy. What you will learn Arguments, subcommands and help Exit codes and error messages Dry run and confirmation for dangerous actions Machine readable output Choosing argparse, click or typer Arguments A good tool has subcommands such as mytool backup run and mytool backup list , options with defaults and help text for each. argparse in the standard library handles this without dependencies, which suits tools installed on servers. Exit codes Return 0 on success and a non zero code on failure so scripts and schedulers can react. Print errors to standard error, and make messages say what failed and what to do next. Do not print a stack trace for an expected problem such as a missing file. Dry run Destructive tools need a --dry-run that prints what would change, and often a required --yes or a typed confirmation for production. Default to the safe option, and show the target environment in the output so mistakes are visible. Output Provide --output json for scripts alongside readable text for people. Write results to standard output and logs to standard error, so piping into other tools works. Keep field names stable, because other tools will depend on them. Libraries argparse is enough for most tools. click and typer give less boilerplate, nicer help and testing helpers, at the cost of a dependency. Pick one per team and wrap your logic in plain functions so the CLI layer stays thin and easy to test. CLI tools with argparse, click and typer Flow in order: 1, Parse arguments to Plan and dry run; 2, Plan and dry run to Apply; 3, Apply to Exit code and output. Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Parse arguments Step What it does: Validate input. If it fails or is weak: Unclear help. Plan and dry run Step What it does: Show changes. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Apply Service What it does: Do the work. If it fails or is weak: Wrong target. Exit code and output Observability What it does: Report result. If it fails or is weak: Always zero. Incident: A cleanup tool that ran against production Incident An engineer meant to test a cleanup in staging, but the default target was production. $ mytool cleanup --older-than 30d target: production deleted 1,204 objects $ echo $? 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The tool defaulted to production and had no dry run, so the first run was real. The team made the environment a required argument, added --dry-run as the default behaviour for destructive commands and printed the target in the first line of output. Common mistakes Defaulting to production Always returning exit code zero Mixing logs and results on standard output Giant functions inside the CLI layer No help text"},"python-ops/api-clients-retries-timeouts-and-pagination":{"html":"\u003cp>Most operations scripts are API clients. Without timeouts and sensible retries they hang, hammer a struggling service or silently miss pages of results.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Always set timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retry with exponential backoff and jitter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotency and which calls are safe to retry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rate limits and the Retry-After header\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pagination without losing items\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts\">Timeouts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A request with no timeout can wait forever and hang a scheduled job. Set a connect timeout and a read timeout, for example \u003ccode>timeout=(3, 15)\u003c/code>. Pick values from the service behaviour, and treat a timeout as a failure to handle, not a surprise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backoff-and-jitter\">Backoff and jitter\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a call fails with a transient error, wait and try again, doubling the wait each time up to a limit and adding random jitter so many clients do not retry in step. Cap the number of attempts and the total time, then fail with a clear error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotency\">Idempotency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>GET\u003c/code> is safe to retry. A \u003ccode>POST\u003c/code> that creates something may create it twice. Use idempotency keys when the API supports them, or check for the result before retrying. Retry only errors that can be transient, such as connection errors, 429 and 5xx, not 400 or 404.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rate-limits\">Rate limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>APIs return 429 and often a \u003ccode>Retry-After\u003c/code> header. Obey it. Slow down proactively by limiting concurrency, and cache results that do not change. A script that ignores limits may get its credentials blocked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pagination\">Pagination\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Large lists arrive in pages. Follow the next page token or link until it is absent, rather than assuming one page is all. Watch for items changing during the walk and for page size limits. Write a generator that yields items so callers do not care about pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"call&gt;retry;retry&gt;page;page&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">API clients: retries, timeouts and pagination\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"call\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"retry\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retry policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"page\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pagination loop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Complete result\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, API call to Retry policy; 2, Retry policy to Pagination loop; 3, Pagination loop to Complete result.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"call\">\u003cdt>API call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Has timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"retry\">\u003cdt>Retry policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Backoff, jitter.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Retry storm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"page\">\u003cdt>Pagination loop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Follows next token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stops at page one.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Complete result \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> All items.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing items.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-report-that-missed-most-of-the-data\">Incident: A report that missed most of the data\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A usage report counted only the first hundred resources because pagination was ignored.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python report.py --count\npython -c &quot;print('next_page_token present: True')&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python report.py --count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">resources: 100\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(&#x27;next_page_token present: True&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The client read one page and ignored the next page token, so the report looked plausible but was wrong. The team replaced it with a generator that follows tokens until none remain and added a test with a fake API that returns three pages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No timeout on requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying non idempotent calls blindly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retrying immediately without backoff\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring Retry-After\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only the first page\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"timeouts","text":"Timeouts"},{"id":"backoff-and-jitter","text":"Backoff and jitter"},{"id":"idempotency","text":"Idempotency"},{"id":"rate-limits","text":"Rate limits"},{"id":"pagination","text":"Pagination"},{"id":"incident-a-report-that-missed-most-of-the-data","text":"Incident: A report that missed most of the data"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Requests advanced usage","u":"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/"},{"t":"urllib3 retry","u":"https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/urllib3.util.html"},{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"Most operations scripts are API clients. Without timeouts and sensible retries they hang, hammer a struggling service or silently miss pages of results. What you will learn Always set timeouts Retry with exponential backoff and jitter Idempotency and which calls are safe to retry Rate limits and the Retry-After header Pagination without losing items Timeouts A request with no timeout can wait forever and hang a scheduled job. Set a connect timeout and a read timeout, for example timeout=(3, 15) . Pick values from the service behaviour, and treat a timeout as a failure to handle, not a surprise. Backoff and jitter When a call fails with a transient error, wait and try again, doubling the wait each time up to a limit and adding random jitter so many clients do not retry in step. Cap the number of attempts and the total time, then fail with a clear error. Idempotency A GET is safe to retry. A POST that creates something may create it twice. Use idempotency keys when the API supports them, or check for the result before retrying. Retry only errors that can be transient, such as connection errors, 429 and 5xx, not 400 or 404. Rate limits APIs return 429 and often a Retry-After header. Obey it. Slow down proactively by limiting concurrency, and cache results that do not change. A script that ignores limits may get its credentials blocked. Pagination Large lists arrive in pages. Follow the next page token or link until it is absent, rather than assuming one page is all. Watch for items changing during the walk and for page size limits. Write a generator that yields items so callers do not care about pages. API clients: retries, timeouts and pagination Flow in order: 1, API call to Retry policy; 2, Retry policy to Pagination loop; 3, Pagination loop to Complete result. Outside party Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. API call Outside party What it does: Has timeout. If it fails or is weak: No timeout. Retry policy Step What it does: Backoff, jitter. If it fails or is weak: Retry storm. Pagination loop Step What it does: Follows next token. If it fails or is weak: Stops at page one. Complete result Data store What it does: All items. If it fails or is weak: Missing items. Incident: A report that missed most of the data Incident A usage report counted only the first hundred resources because pagination was ignored. $ python report.py --count resources: 100 $ python -c \"print(&#x27;next_page_token present: True&#x27;)\" (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The client read one page and ignored the next page token, so the report looked plausible but was wrong. The team replaced it with a generator that follows tokens until none remain and added a test with a fake API that returns three pages. Common mistakes No timeout on requests Retrying non idempotent calls blindly Retrying immediately without backoff Ignoring Retry-After Reading only the first page"},"python-ops/concurrency-threads-asyncio-and-processes":{"html":"\u003cp>Checking a thousand hosts one by one takes forever. Concurrency helps, but the right kind depends on whether the work waits on the network or burns CPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>I/O bound versus CPU bound\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Threads for simple parallel I/O\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>asyncio and the event loop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Processes for CPU work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Limits, errors and cancellation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-kinds-of-work\">Two kinds of work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>I/O bound work waits for networks, disks and APIs, so many tasks can overlap. CPU bound work uses the processor, such as compressing or parsing large data. The global interpreter lock lets only one thread run Python code at a time, so threads speed up I/O but not CPU work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"threads\">Threads\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>ThreadPoolExecutor\u003c/code> runs a function over many inputs with a fixed number of workers and is the simplest way to parallelise blocking calls such as SSH or HTTP. Keep functions independent, return results rather than mutating shared lists and set a worker limit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"asyncio\">asyncio\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>asyncio\u003c/code> runs many tasks on one thread by switching at \u003ccode>await\u003c/code> points. It scales to thousands of connections but needs async libraries, and one blocking call stalls everything. Use \u003ccode>asyncio.gather\u003c/code> to run tasks together and \u003ccode>asyncio.run\u003c/code> to start.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"processes\">Processes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ProcessPoolExecutor\u003c/code> uses separate processes and so separate interpreters, which gives real parallelism for CPU bound work. Arguments and results must be picklable, and starting processes has a cost, so use them for heavy tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-and-errors\">Limits and errors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Unlimited concurrency overloads the target and your own file descriptors. Use a semaphore or the pool size to limit it. Collect exceptions per task so one failure does not hide the others, and set timeouts so a stuck task does not block the run.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"in&gt;pool;pool&gt;work;work&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Concurrency: threads, asyncio and processes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"in\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Work items\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pool\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pool or event loop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"work\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Tasks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Collected results\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Work items to Pool or event loop; 2, Pool or event loop to Tasks; 3, Tasks to Collected results.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"in\">\u003cdt>Work items \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many hosts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unbounded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pool\">\u003cdt>Pool or event loop \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limited workers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"work\">\u003cdt>Tasks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> I/O or CPU.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong model.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Collected results \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Per task errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-health-check-that-opened-too-many-connections\">Incident: A health check that opened too many connections\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A script checking 3000 hosts at once exhausted file descriptors and failed halfway.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ulimit -n\npython check_all.py\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ulimit\u003c/span> -n\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1024\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python check_all.py\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script started every check at once. The team added a semaphore of 50 concurrent checks, set a per host timeout and collected failures into a report instead of stopping at the first exception.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Threads for CPU heavy work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blocking calls inside async code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unlimited concurrency\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared mutable state between threads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeouts per task\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-work","text":"Two kinds of work"},{"id":"threads","text":"Threads"},{"id":"asyncio","text":"asyncio"},{"id":"processes","text":"Processes"},{"id":"limits-and-errors","text":"Limits and errors"},{"id":"incident-a-health-check-that-opened-too-many-connections","text":"Incident: A health check that opened too many connections"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"concurrent.futures","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html"},{"t":"asyncio","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html"},{"t":"venv","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html"}],"text":"Checking a thousand hosts one by one takes forever. Concurrency helps, but the right kind depends on whether the work waits on the network or burns CPU. What you will learn I/O bound versus CPU bound Threads for simple parallel I/O asyncio and the event loop Processes for CPU work Limits, errors and cancellation Two kinds of work I/O bound work waits for networks, disks and APIs, so many tasks can overlap. CPU bound work uses the processor, such as compressing or parsing large data. The global interpreter lock lets only one thread run Python code at a time, so threads speed up I/O but not CPU work. Threads A ThreadPoolExecutor runs a function over many inputs with a fixed number of workers and is the simplest way to parallelise blocking calls such as SSH or HTTP. Keep functions independent, return results rather than mutating shared lists and set a worker limit. asyncio asyncio runs many tasks on one thread by switching at await points. It scales to thousands of connections but needs async libraries, and one blocking call stalls everything. Use asyncio.gather to run tasks together and asyncio.run to start. Processes ProcessPoolExecutor uses separate processes and so separate interpreters, which gives real parallelism for CPU bound work. Arguments and results must be picklable, and starting processes has a cost, so use them for heavy tasks. Limits and errors Unlimited concurrency overloads the target and your own file descriptors. Use a semaphore or the pool size to limit it. Collect exceptions per task so one failure does not hide the others, and set timeouts so a stuck task does not block the run. Concurrency: threads, asyncio and processes Flow in order: 1, Work items to Pool or event loop; 2, Pool or event loop to Tasks; 3, Tasks to Collected results. Outside party Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Work items Outside party What it does: Many hosts. If it fails or is weak: Unbounded. Pool or event loop Step What it does: Limited workers. If it fails or is weak: Too many. Tasks Step What it does: I/O or CPU. If it fails or is weak: Wrong model. Collected results Data store What it does: Per task errors. If it fails or is weak: Lost errors. Incident: A health check that opened too many connections Incident A script checking 3000 hosts at once exhausted file descriptors and failed halfway. $ ulimit -n 1024 $ python check_all.py OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The script started every check at once. The team added a semaphore of 50 concurrent checks, set a per host timeout and collected failures into a report instead of stopping at the first exception. Common mistakes Threads for CPU heavy work Blocking calls inside async code Unlimited concurrency Shared mutable state between threads No timeouts per task"},"python-ops/testing-with-pytest-and-mocking-infrastructure":{"html":"\u003cp>Operations code changes real systems, so it deserves tests more than most. Good tests run in seconds, use fakes and make it safe to change the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>pytest basics and fixtures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parametrised tests for edge cases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fakes and mocks for external systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing time, files and the environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to test and what not to\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pytest-basics\">pytest basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A test is a function whose name starts with \u003ccode>test_\u003c/code> and uses plain \u003ccode>assert\u003c/code>. pytest shows a clear diff when it fails. A fixture is a function that prepares something a test needs, such as a temporary directory, and is passed in by name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parametrisation\">Parametrisation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>@pytest.mark.parametrize\u003c/code> runs one test with many inputs. It suits parsers, validators and size or duration conversions, where edge cases such as empty values, units and negative numbers matter. Name cases so failures read well.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fakes-and-mocks\">Fakes and mocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Separate logic from I/O. Pass a client into functions so a test can give a fake one that returns canned responses. \u003ccode>unittest.mock\u003c/code> and the \u003ccode>monkeypatch\u003c/code> fixture replace calls when needed. Over mocking tests the mock instead of the code, so prefer simple fakes with real behaviour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-files-and-environment\">Time, files and environment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>tmp_path\u003c/code> for files, \u003ccode>monkeypatch.setenv\u003c/code> for environment variables and pass the current time in as an argument or use a clock function you can replace. Tests that depend on the real clock or on a developer machine become flaky.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-test\">What to test\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Test decisions: which resources are selected for deletion, how retries behave, how errors are reported. Add a few integration tests against a disposable environment. Make sure no test can reach production, for example by clearing credentials in the test environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"code&gt;fake;fake&gt;test;test&gt;ci\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Testing with pytest and mocking infrastructure\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"code\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logic functions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fake\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fake client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">pytest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CI gate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Logic functions to Fake client; 2, Fake client to pytest; 3, pytest to CI gate.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"code\">\u003cdt>Logic functions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pure where possible.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mixed with I/O.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fake\">\u003cdt>Fake client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Canned responses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many mocks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>pytest \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Fast tests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow or flaky.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>CI gate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs on each change.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-cleanup-that-deleted-the-wrong-resources\">Incident: A cleanup that deleted the wrong resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change to a selection rule removed resources that were still in use, and no test covered it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pytest -q\npython -c &quot;print('no test covers resources tagged keep=true')&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pytest -q\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">.....\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5 passed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(&#x27;no test covers resources tagged keep=true&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rule for tagged resources had no test, so the change passed. The team wrote parametrised cases for tags, ages and missing fields, ran them with a fake client in CI and required them to pass before any change to the selection logic could be merged.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logic mixed into I/O code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mocks that copy the implementation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that read the real clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that can reach production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tests for deletion logic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pytest-basics","text":"pytest basics"},{"id":"parametrisation","text":"Parametrisation"},{"id":"fakes-and-mocks","text":"Fakes and mocks"},{"id":"time-files-and-environment","text":"Time, files and environment"},{"id":"what-to-test","text":"What to test"},{"id":"incident-a-cleanup-that-deleted-the-wrong-resources","text":"Incident: A cleanup that deleted the wrong resources"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"pytest","u":"https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/"},{"t":"unittest.mock","u":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.mock.html"},{"t":"Python Packaging User Guide","u":"https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"Operations code changes real systems, so it deserves tests more than most. Good tests run in seconds, use fakes and make it safe to change the tool. What you will learn pytest basics and fixtures Parametrised tests for edge cases Fakes and mocks for external systems Testing time, files and the environment What to test and what not to pytest basics A test is a function whose name starts with test_ and uses plain assert . pytest shows a clear diff when it fails. A fixture is a function that prepares something a test needs, such as a temporary directory, and is passed in by name. Parametrisation @pytest.mark.parametrize runs one test with many inputs. It suits parsers, validators and size or duration conversions, where edge cases such as empty values, units and negative numbers matter. Name cases so failures read well. Fakes and mocks Separate logic from I/O. Pass a client into functions so a test can give a fake one that returns canned responses. unittest.mock and the monkeypatch fixture replace calls when needed. Over mocking tests the mock instead of the code, so prefer simple fakes with real behaviour. Time, files and environment Use tmp_path for files, monkeypatch.setenv for environment variables and pass the current time in as an argument or use a clock function you can replace. Tests that depend on the real clock or on a developer machine become flaky. What to test Test decisions: which resources are selected for deletion, how retries behave, how errors are reported. Add a few integration tests against a disposable environment. Make sure no test can reach production, for example by clearing credentials in the test environment. Testing with pytest and mocking infrastructure Flow in order: 1, Logic functions to Fake client; 2, Fake client to pytest; 3, pytest to CI gate. Step Observability Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Logic functions Step What it does: Pure where possible. If it fails or is weak: Mixed with I/O. Fake client Step What it does: Canned responses. If it fails or is weak: Too many mocks. pytest Observability What it does: Fast tests. If it fails or is weak: Slow or flaky. CI gate Service What it does: Runs on each change. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Incident: A cleanup that deleted the wrong resources Incident A change to a selection rule removed resources that were still in use, and no test covered it. $ pytest -q ..... 5 passed $ python -c \"print(&#x27;no test covers resources tagged keep=true&#x27;)\" (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The rule for tagged resources had no test, so the change passed. The team wrote parametrised cases for tags, ages and missing fields, ran them with a fake client in CI and required them to pass before any change to the selection logic could be merged. Common mistakes Logic mixed into I/O code Mocks that copy the implementation Tests that read the real clock Tests that can reach production No tests for deletion logic"},"python-ops/working-with-cloud-and-kubernetes-sdks-with-safe-patterns":{"html":"\u003cp>SDKs make it easy to change a lot of infrastructure with a few lines. Safe patterns keep automation from becoming the largest risk in the estate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Credentials and least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Listing, filtering and dry run before acting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tagging, ownership and audit trails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kubernetes client patterns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails: limits, approvals and kill switches\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"credentials\">Credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automation should use short lived credentials from a role or workload identity, not long lived keys in files. Give it only the permissions it needs, for example read access for a report and delete access only for one tagged set. Separate roles for read and write tools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"list-filter-then-act\">List, filter, then act\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build the list of targets first, print it and apply filters such as tags and age. Run in dry run mode by default. Only then act, one item at a time with logging, and stop if the count is much larger than expected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tags-and-audit\">Tags and audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tag everything automation creates with the owner and the tool. Log each action with the account, the resource and the reason, to a place others can read. When something is deleted unexpectedly, the log answers who and why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubernetes-patterns\">Kubernetes patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The official client loads configuration from a kubeconfig or from the service account inside a pod. Use label selectors, handle API errors and watch for changes with a timeout and a resource version rather than polling in a tight loop. Use server side dry run where available.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cap how many resources one run may change, require an explicit flag or approval for large changes, check that the account and region are the expected ones, and provide a kill switch such as a feature flag file. Alert when automation changes more than normal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"role&gt;list;list&gt;act;act&gt;audit\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cloud and Kubernetes SDKs with safe patterns\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scoped role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"list\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">List and filter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Act with limits\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"audit\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Scoped role to List and filter; 2, List and filter to Act with limits; 3, Act with limits to Audit log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>Scoped role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Least privilege.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Admin keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"list\">\u003cdt>List and filter \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tags and age.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong filter.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Act with limits \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cap and log.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mass deletion.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"audit\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who and why.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No record.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-automation-that-tried-to-delete-half-the-account\">Incident: An automation that tried to delete half the account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup script matched far more resources than intended because a tag filter was empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python cleanup.py --dry-run\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python cleanup.py --dry-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">matched: 4,812 resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">limit per run: 50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">aborting: matched count exceeds the limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An empty tag value matched everything. The run stopped because of the per run limit that the team had added after an earlier near miss. They fixed the filter, added a check that rejects empty values and kept the limit and the dry run default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long lived keys in scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Acting before listing and reviewing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No cap on changes per run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tags or audit logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using admin permissions for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"credentials","text":"Credentials"},{"id":"list-filter-then-act","text":"List, filter, then act"},{"id":"tags-and-audit","text":"Tags and audit"},{"id":"kubernetes-patterns","text":"Kubernetes patterns"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"incident-an-automation-that-tried-to-delete-half-the-account","text":"Incident: An automation that tried to delete half the account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"boto3","u":"https://github.com/boto/boto3"},{"t":"Kubernetes Python client","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes-client/python"},{"t":"IAM best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"}],"text":"SDKs make it easy to change a lot of infrastructure with a few lines. Safe patterns keep automation from becoming the largest risk in the estate. What you will learn Credentials and least privilege Listing, filtering and dry run before acting Tagging, ownership and audit trails Kubernetes client patterns Guardrails: limits, approvals and kill switches Credentials Automation should use short lived credentials from a role or workload identity, not long lived keys in files. Give it only the permissions it needs, for example read access for a report and delete access only for one tagged set. Separate roles for read and write tools. List, filter, then act Build the list of targets first, print it and apply filters such as tags and age. Run in dry run mode by default. Only then act, one item at a time with logging, and stop if the count is much larger than expected. Tags and audit Tag everything automation creates with the owner and the tool. Log each action with the account, the resource and the reason, to a place others can read. When something is deleted unexpectedly, the log answers who and why. Kubernetes patterns The official client loads configuration from a kubeconfig or from the service account inside a pod. Use label selectors, handle API errors and watch for changes with a timeout and a resource version rather than polling in a tight loop. Use server side dry run where available. Guardrails Cap how many resources one run may change, require an explicit flag or approval for large changes, check that the account and region are the expected ones, and provide a kill switch such as a feature flag file. Alert when automation changes more than normal. Cloud and Kubernetes SDKs with safe patterns Flow in order: 1, Scoped role to List and filter; 2, List and filter to Act with limits; 3, Act with limits to Audit log. Security Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Scoped role Security What it does: Least privilege. If it fails or is weak: Admin keys. List and filter Step What it does: Tags and age. If it fails or is weak: Wrong filter. Act with limits Service What it does: Cap and log. If it fails or is weak: Mass deletion. Audit log Observability What it does: Who and why. If it fails or is weak: No record. Incident: An automation that tried to delete half the account Incident A cleanup script matched far more resources than intended because a tag filter was empty. $ python cleanup.py --dry-run matched: 4,812 resources limit per run: 50 aborting: matched count exceeds the limit (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) An empty tag value matched everything. The run stopped because of the per run limit that the team had added after an earlier near miss. They fixed the filter, added a check that rejects empty values and kept the limit and the dry run default. Common mistakes Long lived keys in scripts Acting before listing and reviewing No cap on changes per run No tags or audit logs Using admin permissions for convenience"}});